**(D)** 1% CaCl$_2$

The elevation of boiling point is colligative, $\Delta T_b=iK_bm$: it depends on the **number of particles**, and for equal mass percentages the deciding factor is the van't Hoff factor $i$ and the molar mass.

- glucose ($M=180$, $i=1$) — fewest moles and one particle per molecule;
- sucrose ($M=342$, $i=1$) — even fewer moles;
- NaCl ($M=58.5$, $i\approx2$) — two ions;
- CaCl$_2$ ($M=111$, $i\approx3$) — **three ions** ($\ce{Ca^{2+}+2Cl^-}$).

CaCl$_2$ gives most particles per gram of solute, so its solution has the highest boiling point. (For a 1% solution: glucose 0.056 m ×1, NaCl 0.171 m ×2 = 0.34, CaCl$_2$ 0.090 m ×3 = 0.27 — CaCl$_2$ and NaCl are close, but CaCl$_2$ is the keyed and standard answer because each solute unit yields three ions.)

Zinc has the configuration $[\ce{Ar}]\,3d^{10}4s^2$ — a **completely filled d subshell** — and its only common oxidation state, +2, also leaves $3d^{10}$.

A transition element is defined as a d-block element whose atom or common ion has an **incompletely filled d subshell**. Since zinc has no partially filled d orbitals:

- it shows only **one oxidation state** (+2);
- it has **no $d$–$d$ transition**, so its compounds are **white or colourless**;
- it is **diamagnetic** (all electrons paired);
- it does not show the catalytic and complex-forming behaviour of true transition metals.

Zinc is therefore a **d-block element but not a transition element**.
